<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

    <!--
        REBEL-6DOF-04 SECONDARY gripper (gripper_type:=st3215).

        Rack and pinion, driven by one Waveshare/Feetech ST3215 bus servo wired
        straight to the rover PC over the USB bus-servo adapter - NOT through
        the Teensy that runs the v2 gripper, the drill and the stack light.
        Built from the CAD export "new_gripper.glb";
        scripts/build_gripper_st3215_meshes.py splits that assembly into the
        per-link meshes loaded below and prints every number quoted here, so
        all of it can be regenerated rather than trusted.

        IT IS A DROP-IN SWAP FOR gripper_v2.xacro
        Same igus ReBeL tool flange (six M3.5 on a 43.01 mm bolt circle, same
        0/60/120... clocking), so arm_gripper_base_joint is byte-for-byte the
        one v2 uses.  The DRIVER JOINT KEEPS ITS NAME, gripper_gear_left_joint,
        and its sign convention - q = +0.07 rad closes, q < 0 opens - so the
        SRDF `open`/`closed` group states, gripper_controllers.yaml,
        moveit_controllers.yaml, gamepad.yaml and every cached pose keep
        working with no edit.  gripper_bucket_left_link and
        gripper_bucket_right_link keep their names too, which is what the ACM
        and the grasp stack address.

        The name survives because it is still literally true: the driver joint
        still turns a gear.  What changed is everything behind it.

        HOW IT WORKS
        The servo output shaft IS the pinion - direct drive, no reduction - so
        the joint value below is the servo shaft angle, exactly, with no
        calibration curve in between.  That is the whole reason this gripper is
        easier to command than v2, where the Teensy took a normalised 0-1 float
        and the hardware had no position sensor at all.  The pinion meshes with
        two opposed racks, one per jaw, so the jaws TRANSLATE along +/-X: the
        jaw faces stay parallel and square through the entire stroke, and the
        gap is exactly linear in q.  There is no over-centre point and no
        non-monotonic branch, both of which v2 has.

        THE ONE CONSTANT EVERYTHING ELSE FOLLOWS FROM
            rack_pitch_radius = 10.02676 mm of jaw travel per radian

        It is NOT measured off the tooth radii.  The pinion's tip radius is
        11.985 mm and its root 9.986, which fit no standard tooth form - the
        rack flanks work out at about a 7 deg pressure angle, so these are
        simplified printed teeth and any addendum reasoning about where the
        pitch circle sits is guesswork.  What is exact for any rack and pinion
        that meshes without skipping is that the rack advances one linear pitch
        per pinion angular pitch.  The pinion has 21 teeth (an order-parameter
        fit over the tooth-tip angles scores 0.911 at N=21 against 0.005 at
        every other count from 18 to 27) and the racks' tip lands sit on a
        3.0000 mm pitch, read straight off the exported vertex coordinates.
        So one servo turn is exactly 21 x 3 mm = 63.0 mm of rack, and

            travel per radian = 21 * 3.0000 / (2 * pi) = 10.02676 mm

        STROKE.  0 to 82.9 mm of jaw gap, against v2's 0 to 83 mm - near enough
        the same working range, reached a completely different way:

          jaw gap [mm] against q [rad], exactly linear
            q      +0.07   0.00  -0.20  -0.50  -1.00  -1.57  -2.00  -3.00  -4.065
            gap      0.0    1.4    5.4   11.4   21.5   32.9   41.5   61.6    82.9

          jaw faces meet at z = 207.8 mm from arm_gripper_base_link (the lip);
          the flat shank faces run z = 115 to 155 mm.

        THREE THINGS TO KNOW BEFORE TRUSTING A COMMANDED ANGLE

        1. -1.57 IS NOT FULLY OPEN HERE.  It is on v2, whose joint stops there.
           This mechanism runs to -4.065, and the SRDF `open` group state -
           which is shared text, `cat` into robot_description_semantic, and has
           to stay valid for v2 as well - still says -1.57.  So a MoveIt
           "open" leaves this gripper at a 32.9 mm gap, less than half its
           travel.  That is a valid pose, not a bug, but anything that wants
           the full 82.9 mm has to command the joint limit directly.
           teleop_speeds_st3215.yaml does exactly that for the joystick.

        2. THE JAWS NEST.  The two scoop halves are handed: the right lip runs
           3.000 mm wider than the left so the pair closes into one bowl.  It
           is the LIPS that meet at q = +0.07, at z = 203-208 mm; the flat
           shank faces are still 0.59 mm apart there.  Anything converting an
           object width into a close angle should use the gap table above,
           which is measured at the shank faces, and stop short of +0.07 on
           anything it actually means to hold.

        3. THE SERVO HAS 360 DEG AND THE STROKE WANTS 237 OF THEM.  q spans
           +0.07 to -4.065 = 4.135 rad = 236.9 deg = 2695 of the ST3215's 4096
           steps.  It fits, but only if the servo's zero is placed with that in
           mind - see st3215_gripper_hardware's closed_steps parameter and the
           calibration note in its header.  Unlike v2 this servo does report a
           real position, so a mis-set zero shows up immediately in
           /joint_states rather than being echoed back as if it were fine.
    -->

    <xacro:property name="rack_pitch_radius" value="0.01002676"/>

    <!-- Full open, from the CAD: the left fingertip's shank face runs into the
         -X end wall after 42.8 mm of travel (ray-cast through the wall to find
         which (y,z) cells are solid all the way through, then measured to the
         frontmost blocked jaw point; the right jaw has 54.8 mm, so the left is
         what binds).  2 mm is held back from that, because the wall is a
         printed part and the fingertip meets it flat-on with nothing to guide
         it in. -->
    <xacro:property name="gripper_open_travel" value="0.0408"/>

    <!-- Keep the nominal commands inside Gazebo's physics limits.  When a
         commanded endpoint equals a DART hard stop the mimic-constrained
         joints can wedge there and refuse the following reverse command.  Real
         hardware retains its physical limits.  Same margin gripper_v2.xacro
         uses, for the same reason. -->
    <xacro:property name="gripper_joint_lower_limit"
                    value="${(-gripper_open_travel / rack_pitch_radius) - (0.05 if gripper_hardware_protocol == 'gazebo' else 0.0)}"/>
    <xacro:property name="gripper_joint_upper_limit"
                    value="${0.12 if gripper_hardware_protocol == 'gazebo' else 0.07}"/>

    <!-- The rack joints mimic the driver at +rack_pitch_radius, so their limits
         are the driver's scaled by the same factor.  Reusing the driver limits
         unscaled would make every pose invalid in MoveIt by three orders of
         magnitude. -->
    <xacro:property name="rack_joint_lower_limit"
                    value="${gripper_joint_lower_limit * rack_pitch_radius}"/>
    <xacro:property name="rack_joint_upper_limit"
                    value="${gripper_joint_upper_limit * rack_pitch_radius}"/>

    <!-- Real ST3215 numbers rather than v2's placeholder 500 N.m / 1.0 rad/s.
         Stall torque is 30 kg.cm at 12 V and no-load speed 45 rpm, and the
         pinion is direct-driven, so these ARE the joint's limits with nothing
         in between. The velocity matters: this gripper has to turn 4.135 rad to
         go from closed to fully open where v2 turns 1.64, so at v2's nominal
         1.0 rad/s a full open would take over four seconds. joint_limits.yaml
         allows the gripper 10 rad/s, so this is the binding value. -->
    <xacro:property name="servo_effort" value="2.94"/>
    <xacro:property name="servo_velocity" value="4.71"/>

    <!-- Only Gazebo reads these. Sized the same way gripper_v2.xacro's are:
         damping gives an explicit-integration limit of h < 2I/b well clear of
         the 1 ms world step, and friction stays under each link's own gravity
         load so it cannot weld the mechanism. The racks are prismatic, so
         their friction is a force in newtons, not a torque. -->
    <xacro:property name="pinion_joint_damping" value="0.01"/>
    <xacro:property name="pinion_joint_friction" value="0.001"/>
    <xacro:property name="rack_joint_damping" value="5.0"/>
    <xacro:property name="rack_joint_friction" value="0.05"/>

    <!-- Mass properties integrated from each exported mesh at 1050 kg/m^3
         (near-solid printed plastic), the density gripper_v2.xacro uses so the
         two grippers stay comparable. The servo is the exception: it is
         carried at the ST3215's datasheet 60 g and combined with the mount by
         parallel axis, so the base tensor is not a uniform-density one.

         TOTAL 797 g, against v2's 458 g. Most of that is the mount casting -
         404 cm^3 of modelled solid, 424 g at this density - and the two 104
         cm^3 scoops. If the real parts are printed at a normal infill the true
         figure will be well under this; the density is deliberately the
         conservative one, and it is the number the ReBeL's payload budget
         should be checked against. -->
    <xacro:macro name="gripper_inertial" params="mass cx cy cz ixx iyy izz ixy:=0 ixz:=0 iyz:=0">
        <inertial>
            <origin xyz="${cx} ${cy} ${cz}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <mass value="${mass}"/>
            <inertia ixx="${ixx}" ixy="${ixy}" ixz="${ixz}"
                     iyy="${iyy}" iyz="${iyz}" izz="${izz}"/>
        </inertial>
    </xacro:macro>

    <xacro:macro name="gripper_mesh_geometry" params="file">
        <visual>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/gripper_st3215/${file}"/>
            </geometry>
            <material name="">
                <color rgba="1 1 1 1"/>
            </material>
        </visual>
        <collision>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/gripper_st3215/${file}"/>
            </geometry>
        </collision>
    </xacro:macro>

    <!-- ==================== mount ==================== -->
    <!-- Identical to gripper_v2.xacro's, deliberately: the flange bolt circle
         on this mount is the same radius at the same clocking, so the two
         grippers hang off link6 in the same place and the same orientation and
         nothing that cached a TF between link6 and the gripper has to care
         which one is fitted. -->
    <joint name="arm_gripper_base_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="link6"/>
        <child link="arm_gripper_base_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0522" rpy="0.0 0.0 3.141592653589793"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Mount casting, end walls and the ST3215 case, merged into one solid. -->
    <link name="arm_gripper_base_link">
        <xacro:gripper_inertial mass="0.4843" cx="0.00182" cy="0.00466" cz="0.05230"
                                ixx="8.4368e-04" iyy="2.0360e-03" izz="1.6291e-03"
                                ixy="1.2821e-05" ixz="-2.1053e-05" iyz="1.0487e-04"/>
        <xacro:gripper_mesh_geometry file="st3215_base.stl"/>
    </link>

    <!-- ==================== pinion (the driver) ==================== -->
    <!-- Keeps gripper_v2's name for the joint AND for its child link. That is
         not a compatibility fudge: in both grippers this joint is the one the
         controller commands and its child is the gear it turns.

         AXIS IS -Z, NOT +Z, and that is what makes +q close. The rack that
         meshes on the +Y side of the pinion moves -X when the pinion turns
         about +Z, and that rack carries the LEFT (-X) jaw, so a +Z axis would
         make +q open - backwards from the convention the rest of the stack
         assumes. Flipping the axis is preferred to negating both mimic
         multipliers, so that the two rack joints report the same number and a
         reader comparing joint_states sees one value, not a signed pair. -->
    <link name="gripper_gear_left_link">
        <xacro:gripper_inertial mass="0.0086" cx="0.0" cy="0.0" cz="0.00413"
                                ixx="1.8985e-06" iyy="1.8985e-06" izz="4.4095e-07"/>
        <xacro:gripper_mesh_geometry file="st3215_pinion.stl"/>
    </link>

    <joint name="gripper_gear_left_joint" type="revolute">
        <parent link="arm_gripper_base_link"/>
        <child link="gripper_gear_left_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.083195" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
        <axis xyz="0 0 -1"/>
        <limit effort="${servo_effort}" velocity="${servo_velocity}"
               lower="${gripper_joint_lower_limit}" upper="${gripper_joint_upper_limit}"/>
        <dynamics damping="${pinion_joint_damping}" friction="${pinion_joint_friction}"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- ==================== racks ==================== -->
    <!-- One degree of freedom, carried by the gear mesh. The multiplier is the
         travel-per-radian constant and it is POSITIVE on both sides; the
         opposite sense of the two jaws is in the axis, so both joints report
         the same positive number as they close.

         WHICH CAD SOLID CARRIES WHICH JAW IS NOT WHAT THE NAMES SAY. The solid
         named "Rack" in the export has its teeth on +Y and its carrier block
         under the -X fingertip, so it is the LEFT rack here; "Rack 2" is the
         mirror. The build script asserts the assignment by carrier position
         rather than by name, because getting it backwards silently flips the
         whole open/close convention while still looking like a working
         gripper. -->
    <xacro:macro name="rack" params="side sx cx cy ixy ixz iyz">
        <link name="gripper_rack_${side}_link">
            <xacro:gripper_inertial mass="0.0426" cx="${cx}" cy="${cy}" cz="0.10412"
                                    ixx="1.8332e-05" iyy="5.2456e-05" izz="6.7720e-05"
                                    ixy="${ixy}" ixz="${ixz}" iyz="${iyz}"/>
            <xacro:gripper_mesh_geometry file="st3215_rack_${side}.stl"/>
        </link>

        <joint name="gripper_rack_${side}_joint" type="prismatic">
            <parent link="arm_gripper_base_link"/>
            <child link="gripper_rack_${side}_link"/>
            <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
            <axis xyz="${sx} 0 0"/>
            <limit effort="${servo_effort / rack_pitch_radius}" velocity="${servo_velocity * rack_pitch_radius}"
                   lower="${rack_joint_lower_limit}" upper="${rack_joint_upper_limit}"/>
            <dynamics damping="${rack_joint_damping}" friction="${rack_joint_friction}"/>
            <mimic joint="gripper_gear_left_joint" multiplier="${rack_pitch_radius}" offset="0.0"/>
        </joint>
    </xacro:macro>

    <xacro:rack side="left"  sx="1"  cx="0.00338"  cy="0.00716"
                ixy="-9.6137e-06" ixz="5.1487e-06"  iyz="2.0759e-06"/>
    <xacro:rack side="right" sx="-1" cx="-0.00338" cy="-0.00838"
                ixy="-8.5631e-06" ixz="-5.1487e-06" iyz="-1.8410e-06"/>

    <!-- ==================== fingertips ==================== -->
    <!-- Bolted to their racks, so these joints are fixed - there is no
         fingertip degree of freedom to cancel the way v2's -1.0 mimic cancels
         its bar. They are separate links only so that gripper_bucket_*_link
         keeps addressing the jaw the way it does on v2: that is the name the
         ACM, the grasp stack and the held-probe check all use.

         Their frames are arm_gripper_base_link's frame at q = 0, so every
         number below reads directly in base coordinates at the zero pose.

         COLLISION IS BOXES, AND IT HAS TO STAY THAT WAY. In this gz-sim /
         DART-bullet stack a <mesh> collision generates NO contacts against
         another <mesh>: bullet wraps both as concave GImpact shapes and
         GImpact-vs-GImpact yields no manifold, which is why the v2 fingers
         passed through everything they were asked to operate. Four boxes per
         tip, z-bands tiling the mesh end to end so there is no gap, x/y from a
         300k-point surface sampling. The scoop bands are SOLID where the real
         part is a shell - the same conservative trade gripper_v2.xacro makes,
         and for the same reason: nothing in these worlds is scooped as
         particles. -->
    <xacro:macro name="fingertip" params="side sx b0x b3x b3sx">
        <link name="gripper_bucket_${side}_link">
            <xacro:gripper_inertial mass="0.1091" cx="${sx * 0.02098}" cy="-0.00083" cz="0.15027"
                                    ixx="1.0192e-04" iyy="8.3559e-05" izz="5.4152e-05"
                                    ixz="${sx * 8.0858e-06}"/>
            <visual>
                <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <geometry>
                    <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/gripper_st3215/st3215_bucket_${side}.stl"/>
                </geometry>
                <material name="">
                    <color rgba="1 1 1 1"/>
                </material>
            </visual>
            <collision name="tip_band0">
                <origin xyz="${sx * 0.01700} -0.00100 0.12660" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <geometry><box size="0.0320 0.0775 0.0232"/></geometry>
            </collision>
            <collision name="tip_band1">
                <origin xyz="${sx * 0.02600} -0.00075 0.14980" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <geometry><box size="0.0500 0.0600 0.0232"/></geometry>
            </collision>
            <collision name="tip_band2">
                <origin xyz="${sx * 0.02600} -0.00075 0.17300" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <geometry><box size="0.0500 0.0600 0.0232"/></geometry>
            </collision>
            <collision name="tip_band3">
                <origin xyz="${sx * b3x} -0.00075 0.19620" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <geometry><box size="${b3sx} 0.0600 0.0232"/></geometry>
            </collision>
        </link>

        <joint name="gripper_bucket_${side}_joint" type="fixed">
            <parent link="gripper_rack_${side}_link"/>
            <child link="gripper_bucket_${side}_link"/>
            <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
        </joint>
    </xacro:macro>

    <!-- The top band is where the handedness shows: the right lip is 43.0 mm
         wide against the left's 40.0, which is what lets the two nest. Do not
         "tidy" them to match. -->
    <xacro:fingertip side="left"  sx="-1" b0x="0.01700" b3x="0.02101" b3sx="0.0400"/>
    <xacro:fingertip side="right" sx="1"  b0x="0.01700" b3x="0.01951" b3sx="0.0430"/>

    <!-- ==================== TCP ==================== -->
    <!-- Held at gripper_v2's 0.15 m so the SRDF chain and every cached pose
         stay valid across a gripper swap. It is a reference frame, not the
         contact point: the jaws span z = 115 to 208 mm and meet at 208. -->
    <link name="gripper_tcp"/>

    <joint name="gripper_tcp_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="arm_gripper_base_link"/>
        <child link="gripper_tcp"/>
        <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.15" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Gripper camera (D435i). The bracket is modelled in neither base mesh,
         but this mount's camera holes sit 18.2 mm further out in +Y than v2's
         (65.27 mm from the flange axis against 47.08), so the pose is NOT the
         one gripper_camera.xacro carries by default and is overridden here. -->
    <xacro:property name="gripper_camera_y" value="0.065274"/>
    <xacro:include filename="gripper_camera.xacro" />

</robot>
